#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124112 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e593685-f5ca-5501-94bf-7cb4d49e935b" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Involving Reciprocals and Radicals"}] :question/id #uuid "a2c947c7-9873-5137-91ca-4fe0b42e9bb7" :question/math-academy-id "e-11021" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z = \\dfrac {\\sqrt {y+1}}{x+y}$." :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{y + 1 \\ge 0; x + y \\ne 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, y \\geq -1, \\: y \\neq -x \\big\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124113 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4fd256-a726-523c-84dc-f62d4e2711cc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Domain of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "42cbeba7-685e-5782-9695-9671d16b7f80" :question/math-academy-id "e-11022" :question/problem "Sketch the domain of $g(x,y)=\\dfrac{1}{\\ln(x+y)}$." :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{x + y > 0; \\ln (x + y) \\ne 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD = \\left \\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,: \\, y \\gt -x, \\, y \\neq 1-x \\right \\}\n$$\n\nNow, we plot the corresponding domain.\n\n- Starting with $y> -x$, we draw a dashed line $y=-x$ and shade the region *above* the line.\n- Then, we draw a dashed line $y=1-x$ through the shaded region.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1899/Source/Images/bcd5261dc88387b9f76d0ff643039167.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124114 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9289c4d-4f7f-5704-a2cb-272f92d8fef3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "59fa0287-0562-5cf2-8469-946a69dc8adf" :question/math-academy-id "e-11025" :question/problem "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n$f(x,y) = y^2 - \\log_3(x - 7)$.\nEvaluate this function at the point $(10,3)$." :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(10,3)$, we substitute $x = 10$ and $y = 3$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(10, 3) &= (y^{2} - \\log_{3} (x - 7)) \\mid _{(10, 3)} \\\\\n&= 3^{2} - \\log_{3} (10 - 7) \\\\\n&= 9 - \\log_{3} (3) \\\\\n&= 9 - 1 \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(10,3)=8\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124116 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f2bc84-03e8-5921-81e2-683a09195279" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Containing Exponential, Logarithmic, and Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "bdae9fe0-a491-531b-ac36-7a37d2c32f16" :question/math-academy-id "e-11029" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z= \\dfrac{\\ln (2x+y)}{\\sqrt{x}}$." :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive,\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n${2x + y > 0; x > 0$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\big\\{(x, y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, 2x+y >0, \\: x > 0 \\big\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f2bc84-03e8-5921-81e2-683a09195279" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Containing Exponential, Logarithmic, and Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ab18917-d118-537c-ba2c-0019eef305da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: x + y > 0, y > 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121310} {:answer/id #uuid "061c8f9b-5d90-51c7-93fe-5fcbf78738e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: x + y > 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121311} {:answer/id #uuid "45e31821-954b-5468-8ae1-98d19db3c4b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121312} {:answer/id #uuid "761e8907-452b-5e2f-b048-5c5a6d10ed52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: x + y \\ge 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121313} {:answer/id #uuid "76cd8e3e-7787-5f79-8fc3-c4d6a6a804d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: x + y > 0, y \\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76cd8e3e-7787-5f79-8fc3-c4d6a6a804d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: x + y > 0, y \\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121314} :answer-field/id #uuid "94f50ade-4552-58dd-a8a7-e1fd7b897ae1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121309}] :question/id #uuid "1a0dae25-b4dd-5e0a-a533-db76e6eac14c" :question/math-academy-id "q-158180" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z = (\\ln (x + y))/(y)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f2bc84-03e8-5921-81e2-683a09195279" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Containing Exponential, Logarithmic, and Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4a72dc0-014e-5258-a6e2-cedbaa689497" :answer/